course: dictionary
lesson: 10998
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The sentence around it tells you which meaning is used. Look at the clue words, just as you would with any new word.
explain: «And some economists really do argue that home machines like washers freed more time than the internet.» Here a washer is a washing machine, because the words «home machines» are the clue.
explain: «A tap that drips once a second wastes thousands of litres a year, just because a small washer needs changing.» Here a washer is a small ring, not a machine. The clues are «tap», «drips» and «small».
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence. In Australia, a washer can even mean a small face cloth, so always check the country and the clues.
fact: A washer can be a small flat ring of metal, rubber or plastic.
fact: Washers go under a nut or a screw, or inside a tap, to make it tight or stop leaks.
fact: Informally, a washer can mean a washing machine or a dishwasher.
fact: A «window washer» or «car washer» is a person who washes things.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, a washer is a small square cloth for washing your face. In the UK it is called a flannel.
exercise: In «a tap that drips, because a small washer needs changing», what is a washer? | A washing machine | A face cloth | *A small ring that stops leaks | A person who fixes taps
exercise: In «home machines like washers freed more time», what is a washer? | A small metal ring | A face cloth | A window cleaner | *A washing machine
exercise: What does «window washer» mean? | *A person who washes windows | A face cloth | A ring under a nut | A washing machine
test: In Australia, what can a washer mean? | *A small square face cloth | A dishwasher | A nut and screw | A tap ring
test: Which sentence uses washer to mean a machine? | Ella cleaned the car with a sponge | *The washer and dryer are in the kitchen | A small washer under the nut | A rubber washer stopped the leak
exam: Ella reads «a small washer needs changing» and «home machines like washers». What is the difference? | Both mean a machine | *The first is a small ring and the second is a machine | The first is a person and the second is a tap | Both mean a face cloth
